Assignment Overview

This submission contains the implementation and practice of important Object-Oriented Programming concepts covered in Week 3.

The assignment focuses on classes and objects, constructors, encapsulation, getters and setters, object references, method calls, arithmetic operations, copy constructors, and stack and heap visualization.

---

1. Point and Triangle

Folder: "PointTriangle"

Point.java

The "Point" class represents a point using two coordinates.

The Point class includes:

- Private "x" and "y" attributes
- No-argument constructor
- Parameterized constructor
- Copy constructor
- Getters and setters
- Point addition
- Point subtraction
- Distance calculation
- "toString()" method

Point Operations

The class supports addition and subtraction of two Point objects and calculates the distance between two points.

The copy constructor is also used to create an independent copy of a Point object.

---

Triangle.java

The "Triangle" class represents a triangle using three Point objects.

The Triangle class includes:

- Three Point attributes: "p1", "p2", and "p3"
- Default constructor
- Parameterized constructor
- Copying Point objects inside the constructor
- Getters and setters
- Perimeter calculation
- Area calculation
- Triangle type detection
- "toString()" method

The triangle is created using three Point objects and its perimeter, area, and type can be displayed.

---

Main.java

The driver program demonstrates:

- Creating Point objects
- Point addition
- Point subtraction
- Copy constructor
- Using "toString()"
- Changing a Point object using setters
- Creating a Triangle
- Calculating Triangle perimeter
- Calculating Triangle area
- Detecting the Triangle type

---

2. Stack and Heap Visualization

Folder: "StackHeapVisuals"

This section demonstrates how Java objects and references work in memory.

The visualization shows:

- The "main" stack frame
- Point object references stored in the stack
- Point objects stored in the heap
- References from stack variables to heap objects
- Objects created during Point addition
- Copy constructor and object independence

The Stack and Heap diagram helps demonstrate the relationship between object references and actual objects in memory.

---

3. ComplexNumber

Folder: "ComplexNumber"

ComplexNumber.java

The "ComplexNumber" class represents a complex number using real and imaginary parts.

The class includes:

- Private "real" attribute
- Private "imaginary" attribute
- Default constructor
- Parameterized constructor
- Copy constructor
- Getters and setters
- Addition
- Subtraction
- Multiplication
- "toString()" method

Complex Number Operations

The class performs addition, subtraction, and multiplication between two ComplexNumber objects.

The copy constructor is also used to create a separate copy of an existing complex number.

---

Main.java

The driver demonstrates:

- Creating ComplexNumber objects
- Displaying complex numbers
- Addition
- Subtraction
- Multiplication
- Copy constructor
- Using setters
- Showing that a copied object is independent

---

4. RationalNumber

Folder: "RationalNumber"

RationalNumber.java

The "RationalNumber" class represents a rational number using numerator and denominator.

The class includes:

- Private numerator attribute
- Private denominator attribute
- Default constructor
- Parameterized constructor
- Copy constructor
- Getters and setters
- Fraction simplification
- Addition
- Subtraction
- "toString()" method
- GCD helper method
- Zero-denominator handling

Rational Number Operations

The class can simplify fractions and perform addition and subtraction between rational numbers.

It also handles special cases such as a zero denominator and zero numerator.

---

Main.java

The driver demonstrates:

- Creating RationalNumber objects
- Normal fraction operations
- Fraction simplification
- Copy constructor
- Addition
- Subtraction
- Zero-denominator handling
- Zero numerator case

